SPURS HLE and SPU runtime: workloads, wake-ups, thread images, diagnostics.

- cellSpurs: every taskset is its own workload (unique wid, bit in wklEnabled, taskset EA in wklInfo1[wid].arg), which
  is where a pushed LFQueue entry finds the task to signal (src/spurs_lfqueue.cpp).
- A task parked in WAIT_SIGNAL wakes on queue data (spu_channels, spu_workload).
- Taskset generations: a taskset shut down and created again at the same address leaves its old tasks parked in
  WAIT_SIGNAL; cellSpursShutdownTaskset bumps the address's generation and a task of an older one ends itself
  instead of answering the new taskset's signals or clearing its task bits.
- spu_register_ls_hook, SPU_GETLOG=<lo>,<hi> (log MFC transfers touching an LS range), lr in the "synthesised stop"
  message.
- The WAIT_SIGNAL wait and wake share one lock/broadcast/timed-wait helper set (spu_workload.c).
- mfc_for reads SPU_PCSAMPLE once (toolkit v0.17.0 looked it up on every MFC channel access while it was unset;
  getenv walks the environment, and the DSP thread of the game's audio engine ran at half speed: about every
  second audio block was silent in a fight, and the sound was choppy and rough).

